course: dictionary
lesson: 9287
title: Sparklers and context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sparkler is a thin wire stick covered in a firework mix. When you light it, it burns slowly and throws off bright sparks. The words around it give you clues: «Light a sparkler» tells you to set it alight, and «wave a sparkler» tells you to move it about.
explain: Sparkler can also mean something that sparkles, like a shiny jewel or a sparkling drink. To decide which meaning fits, read the words around it. If the sentence is about fire, gloves or a bucket of water, it means the firework.
explain: «Sparklers are held with gloves at arm's length and put into a bucket of water when they finish.» The words gloves, arm's length and bucket of water are safety words, so this sentence is about the firework. Always swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it makes sense.
fact: A sparkler is a thin wire stick covered with a firework mix that burns slowly.
fact: Sparklers are used on Bonfire Night, on Independence Day, at New Year, at Diwali and on birthday cakes.
fact: «Sparklers burn much hotter than a candle flame.» Their wire stays hot after the sparks have stopped.
fact: Hold sparklers at arm's length, one at a time, wear gloves, and never wave them near other people.
fact: Children under five should not hold sparklers.
exercise: What is a sparkler? | A small pink flower | *A thin wire stick covered in a firework mix that throws off bright sparks | A shiny jewel on a ring | A kind of cake with candles on top
exercise: «Put used sparklers into a bucket of water.» Why must you do this? | *Because the wire stays hot after it has burned | To make them sparkle more | To make them grow bigger | Because water makes them quieter
exercise: «Sparklers are held with gloves at arm's length.» Find and copy one word that tells you how to keep safe. | sparks | sparklers | *gloves | bright
test: «Sparklers burn hotter than a cooking flame.» Why are they only for older children? | They only work after dark | They are too heavy to lift | *They can burn skin and set clothes on fire | They make a very loud bang
test: What should you do with a finished sparkler? | Put it in your pocket | *Put it in a bucket of water or sand | Leave it on the grass | Throw it at a friend
exam: «Fireworks and sparklers can cause bad burns; a sparkler burns much hotter than a candle flame.» What is the main message of this sentence? | Candles are more dangerous than sparklers | *Sparklers can hurt you, so handle them with care | Fireworks should be lit indoors | Sparklers are only pretty to look at
